#pragma once
// Built-in Library cards. Built-in reference cards (UK starter pack 2), from MESHDECK 1.x. Edit with care: each card names its sources.
namespace meshdeck {

struct CoreCard { const char* title; const char* scope; const char* body; const char* sources; };

static const char* const kLibraryEdition = "UK starter pack 2";
static const char* const kLibraryReviewed = "2026-10-31";
static const char* const kLibraryNote = "Rewritten as UK-wide content on 2026-10-31. Recheck every number and source against the official pages before relying on this pack.";
static const CoreCard kCoreCards[] = {
    {"UK EMERGENCY CONTACTS", "United Kingdom; some services differ between Great Britain and Northern Ireland", "Immediate danger or life-threatening illness: 999 (112 also works).\nUrgent medical help that is not life-threatening: NHS 111 in Great Britain. In Northern Ireland, contact your GP or the out-of-hours service.\nPolice, non-emergency: 101.\nPower cut: 105 in Great Britain. In Northern Ireland, contact NIE Networks.\nSmell of gas in Great Britain: 0800 111 999. Record the Northern Ireland gas emergency number if you live there.\nFlood information: Floodline 0345 988 1188 (Great Britain). Northern Ireland flooding incident line: 0300 2000 100.\nDeaf, hard of hearing or speech-impaired: register in advance with emergencySMS so you can text 999.\nWater supply problems: use the 24-hour number on your water bill.\nThese are contact references, not a guarantee that telephone networks or services are available. No shelter is confirmed by this pack.", "https://www.nhs.uk/nhs-services/urgent-and-emergency-care-services/when-to-use-111/\nhttps://www.powercut105.com/\nhttps://www.gov.uk/help-during-flood"},
    {"THREE-DAY SUPPLIES", "UK household preparedness", "Build a household kit gradually and check it every six months. Plan for three days of disruption.\nStore at least 3 litres of drinking water per person per day; cooking and washing need additional water. Keep familiar shelf-stable food, a manual tin opener, torch and batteries, warm clothing, blankets, essential medicines, first-aid supplies and hygiene items.\nInclude a charged power bank, battery or wind-up FM radio, paper contacts, essential document copies, and cash suitable for your household. Consider baby supplies, pet needs and anyone relying on powered medical equipment.\nRecord local radio frequencies before an outage. Keep essential checklists on paper too.", "https://beready.campaign.gov.uk/be-ready-for-emergencies/"},
    {"FLOOD SAFETY", "United Kingdom; check each property and route", "Check the long-term flood risk for each exact property and planned route with the official flood service for where you live; a town name alone does not establish flood risk. Sign up for flood warnings where available. Floodline (Great Britain): 0345 988 1188. Northern Ireland flooding incident line: 0300 2000 100.\nBefore forecast flooding, prepare your kit, charge devices and move medicines and important documents higher if safe. Plan help for anyone who cannot leave independently.\nFollow emergency-service instructions and evacuate when told. Do not walk, cycle or drive through floodwater. It can hide hazards and contamination. Never touch electrical switches while standing in water.\nOnly return when authorities say it is safe. Have flooded electrical systems checked before restoring power. This offline card contains no current warnings.", "https://www.gov.uk/help-during-flood\nhttps://www.gov.uk/check-long-term-flood-risk"},
    {"POWER AND CO SAFETY", "UK household safety", "Report a power cut on 105 (Great Britain); in Northern Ireland contact NIE Networks. Use torches and conserve batteries. Avoid damaged cables and electrical equipment.\nNever use a barbecue or camping stove indoors or inside a tent. Never run a generator or engine in an enclosed space. Outdoor generators must be positioned according to the manufacturer's instructions so exhaust cannot enter occupied spaces.\nCarbon monoxide cannot be detected by smell. If exposure is suspected, get outside, seek medical advice and do not re-enter until advised it is safe. Call 999 for breathing difficulty, sudden confusion, collapse, weakness or chest pain after suspected exposure.\nUse suitable carbon monoxide alarms. Do not improvise indoor heating with outdoor cooking equipment.", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/carbon-monoxide-poisoning/\nhttps://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/hazardous-substances/carbon-monoxide.htm\nhttps://www.powercut105.com/"},
    {"WATER INTERRUPTION", "UK water supply notices", "Your water supplier's 24-hour number is on your bill and its website. Record it before an emergency: [not set]. Follow the notice issued for your supply; do not assume that restored water is ready to drink.\nA boil-water notice differs from a do-not-use notice. Under a boil-water notice, bring water to a rolling boil, then allow it to cool naturally before drinking or relevant food preparation. Use it for tooth brushing and ice too. Avoid scalds and keep containers clean.\nIf instructed not to use the water, use bottled water or the alternative supply arranged by the provider. Boiling is not permission to disregard a do-not-use notice.\nThis card does not establish that river water or floodwater is safe to drink.", "https://www.dwi.gov.uk/receiving-a-boil-water-notice/"},
    {"FOOD IN A POWER CUT", "UK food safety summary", "Keep fridge and freezer doors closed. A closed fridge may retain a safe temperature for about four hours; a full freezer about 48 hours and a half-full freezer about 24 hours. These are estimates, not guarantees.\nUse a thermometer: normal fridge storage should be 5C or colder. Do not keep perishable food with an unknown or unsafe temperature history; if in doubt, throw it out.\nKeep raw and ready-to-eat foods separate. Use clean hands, surfaces and utensils. Ready-to-use infant formula is the option when water cannot be boiled safely.\nRead the full official guidance when planning your food arrangements.", "https://www.food.gov.uk/"},
    {"COLD AND HYPOTHERMIA", "UK first-aid summary; adults and children", "Hypothermia is a medical emergency. Signs can include shivering, cold pale skin, slurred speech, slow breathing, unusual tiredness or confusion. Call 999 if you suspect it.\nWhile waiting, move the person into shelter, remove wet clothing and wrap them in dry blankets or a sleeping bag, covering the head. Stay with them. Give a warm non-alcoholic drink and sugary food only if fully awake and able to swallow safely.\nDo not use a hot bath, hot-water bottle or heat lamp. Do not rub limbs or give alcohol.\nThis is a short reference for use alongside first-aid training, not a diagnosis. Follow emergency-call instructions when available.", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/hypothermia/"},
    {"ADULT CPR AND AED", "ADULT ONLY; RCUK 2025 summary", "Make sure it is safe to approach. If an adult is unresponsive, call 999 immediately, use speakerphone and assess breathing while the call connects. Ask someone to fetch an AED. Gasping is not normal breathing.\nIf unresponsive and not breathing normally, start chest compressions in the centre of the chest: 100-120 per minute, 5-6 cm deep, with full recoil and minimal interruptions.\nIf trained and able, give 30 compressions then 2 rescue breaths. Otherwise give continuous chest compressions.\nSwitch on the AED and follow its instructions. Nobody should touch the person during analysis or a shock; resume CPR when prompted. Follow the call handler and continue until professional help takes over, normal signs of life return, or you cannot continue.\nChildren and drowning require additional guidance; this adult card is not their complete protocol.", "https://www.resus.org.uk/professional-library/2025-resuscitation-guidelines/adult-basic-life-support-guidelines"},
    {"HELP IN THE HILLS", "UK mountain, moorland and coastal rescue", "For an emergency in the UK hills, call 999, ask for Police, then Mountain Rescue. At the coast or at sea, call 999 and ask for the Coastguard.\nGive your location, ideally a six-figure grid reference or a named feature; number of people; injuries; equipment available; and callback numbers. Follow the team's instructions and conserve phone power.\nBefore travel, plan for the weather, carry a map and compass you know how to use, and leave a route card and return time with a responsible person. Tell them when safely back.\nA phone GPS position can work without mobile data, but calling or messaging needs a usable network. The Tab5 currently has no working navigation or rescue-calling function. LoRa messages are not an emergency-service dispatch connection.", "https://www.mountain.rescue.org.uk/"},
    {"COLD-WATER SURVIVAL", "RNLI Float to Live summary", "If you unexpectedly fall into cold water, resist the urge to swim immediately. Float while getting control of your breathing.\nTilt your head back with your ears in the water. Relax and breathe normally. Use your hands to help you stay afloat; spread arms and legs if useful. Legs sinking is normal and everyone floats differently.\nOnce breathing is controlled, call for help or swim to safety if able.\nPractise floating in a supervised pool, not by deliberately entering dangerous cold water.\nDo not enter floodwater to test this advice or attempt an untrained rescue. This card is an emergency reminder, not a substitute for water-safety training.", "https://rnli.org/water-safety/float"},
    {"HOUSEHOLD COMMS PLAN", "FIELD OS planning template; not official operational guidance", "Fill this plan before an outage:\nPrimary household contact: [not set]\nOut-of-area contact: [not set]\nSafe nearby meeting point: [not set]\nAlternative meeting point: [not set]\nCheck-in times and timezone: [not set]\nMesh system / channel / region: [not set]\nRadio callsigns or node names: [not set]\nTrusted news radio frequencies: [not set]\nWho checks on vulnerable neighbours: [not set]\nIf a check-in is missed: [agree actions]\n\nTest the plan without internet. Agree what messages mean and distinguish sent-to-radio from acknowledged delivery. Keep a paper copy. Do not publish private channel keys or personal medical details in a shared library.", "Original planning template."},
    {"LOCAL EVACUATION PLAN", "FIELD OS personal planning template", "Create a separate plan for each property. No safe route, shelter or assembly point has been verified by this pack.\nAddress and postcode: [not set]\nFlood risk checked for property and route: [not done]\nPrimary safe destination: [not confirmed]\nAlternative safe destination: [not confirmed]\nRoute avoiding water and closed roads: [not checked]\nTransport and mobility assistance: [not arranged]\nMedicines / glasses / documents / pets: [list]\nUtility isolation points: [identify safely]\nPeople who need notice or assistance: [list]\n\nUse current local authority and emergency-service instructions during an incident. A saved route may be unsafe after flooding or storm damage. Do not interpret a library listing as an open refuge.", "Original planning template."},
};
static const int kCoreCardCount = sizeof(kCoreCards) / sizeof(kCoreCards[0]);

} // namespace meshdeck
